{ New plan: the minority attack. Two pawns against three: push b5 and his c pawn is weak for ever. First, stop c5. [%cal Yc3e2] }
17. Ne2 { Knight to e2: it guards d4, so his c5 break no longer works. Now b5 comes. [%cal Gb4b5][%csl Gd4] } (17. Rfd1 { Also good: the rook first, the knight next. } 17... a6 18. Ne2 g6 { The same plan, a move later. }) (17. Rfc1 { The rook eyes c6, but his knight lands. } 17... Nc4 18. Bxc4 dxc4 { You had to give the bishop. Still better. [%csl Rc4] }) (17. a4 { A pawn move, and the knight lands on c4. } 17... Nc4 18. Nxc4 dxc4 { Still a bit better. }) (17. b5 { Too soon: his break is ready. } 17... c5 18. dxc5 Qxc5 { He has freed himself. Only a little better. [%csl Gc5] }) 17... a6 { He stops b5 with a6. So bring the a pawn: a4, then a5 and b5. [%cal Ya2a4] } 18. a4 { Pawn to a4. b5 is coming anyway, and after a5 his b6 knight has to move. [%cal Ga4a5,Gb4b5] } (18. Rfe1 { Also good. The rook first. } 18... g6 19. a4 Ra8 { The same plan, a move later. }) (18. a3 { The pawn goes one step only. Fine, but slower. } 18... Ra8 19. a4 Bd7 { Two moves for one. }) (18. Rfd1 { Fine too. } 18... Ng6 19. a4 Ra8 { The same plan. }) (18. b5 { The break with no support, and the pawn is lost. } 18... axb5 19. Nb3 Ra8 { A pawn gone. Worse. [%csl Rb5] }) 1-0


