{ The minority attack is not the only plan in this structure. Your bishop sits on g2. Which break suits it? }
19. f3 { Pawn to f3, and e4 next. The bishop on g2 wakes up, and the centre opens on your terms. [%cal Ge3e4][%csl Gg2] } (19. Qb4 { The queen dances, and nothing changes. } 19... Qd8 20. Qd2 Ne4 { Only a little better. }) (19. h4 { A pawn move on the edge. } 19... h6 20. Qb4 Qd8 { Only a little better. }) (19. h3 { Slow. } 19... g5 20. Ne2 Bf5 { Only a little better. }) (19. b4 { The minority attack, but his bishop comes out and your pawns freeze. } 19... Bf5 20. Qb3 Rac8 { Equal. [%csl Rb4] }) (19. Nxd5 { The knight takes, and the pawn takes back. } 19... cxd5 { A knight for a pawn. [%csl Rd5] }) 19... g5 { He kicks the knight. Where does it go? } 20. Nd3 { Knight to d3: it guards e5 and stays near the action. Now e4. [%cal Ge3e4] } (20. Ne2 { Also fine. The knight comes back via c3. } 20... Bf5 21. Nc3 g4 { Still better. }) (20. Nh3 { The rim, and he trades it off. } 20... Bxh3 21. Bxh3 Re8 { Your bishop is on h3, far from e4. Equal. }) (20. e4 { The break with the knight hanging. } 20... gxf4 21. e5 Qd8 { A knight gone. Worse. [%csl Rf4] }) (20. Nxd5 { The knight takes, and the pawn takes back. } 20... cxd5 21. Rxc7 Qxc7 { Rooks traded, and you are a knight down. Lost. }) 20... Be6 { His bishop comes out. Now the break. } 21. e4 { Pawn to e4! The centre opens, and the bishop on g2 sees the whole board. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gg2d5] } (21. Qb4 { The queen retreats, and he untangles. } 21... Ne8 22. Qd2 h6 { Only a little better. }) (21. Nf2 { Slow. } 21... Rd8 22. Qc2 Qe7 { Only a little better. }) (21. Qb3 { The queen steps back. } 21... Rac8 22. Qc3 Nd7 { Only a little better. }) (21. Qc2 { Slow. } 21... Rac8 22. Qc3 Nd7 { Only a little better. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the minority attack, when it is over"]
