{ You are a pawn up with an ugly structure. The temptation is to fix the pawns. Do not. Make something of what you have. }
17. O-O { He castles. Your pawns are doubled and your king is on g8. What is the plan? } 17... Nc6 { Develop: the knight comes out, and the structure stays compact. The king walks up next. [%csl Gc6][%cal Gg8f8,Gf8e7] } (17... Kf8 { Also good: the king first, the knight next. } 18. Rd1 Nc6 { The same plan. }) (17... Bd7 { Also good. } 18. Rd1 Bc6 { Solid. }) (17... b5 { A pawn move, and his rook comes in. } 18. b3 Nc6 19. Rc1 { Still better, but less. }) (17... e5 { You fix your doubled pawn, and give him d5 and the diagonal. } 18. Ne3 Be6 19. Bc4 { His pieces are alive. Only a little better. [%csl Rd5] }) 18. Rd1 { His rook takes the open column. Now the king. } 18... Kf8 { The king steps up. In an endgame the king is a piece: it goes to e7 and d6. [%cal Gf8e7] } (18... Rb8 { The rook moves, and the king waits. } 19. Rd3 Kf8 { Still better. }) (18... b5 { A pawn move, and his rook lands. } 19. Rd6 Bb7 { Still better, but less. [%csl Rd6] }) (18... e5 { You give up d5 again. } 19. Rd5 Be6 20. Rxc5 { A pawn gone. Only a little better. }) (18... Kg7 { The wrong way: the king belongs in the centre. } 19. f4 Kf8 { You turn round. Still better. }) 19. Kf1 { His king comes too. Keep walking. } 19... Ke7 { The king on e7, then d6. Your extra pawn will count later, ugly or not. [%csl Ge7] } (19... b6 { Also good. The bishop comes to b7. } 20. Rd3 Bb7 { Still better. }) (19... b5 { Fine, a bit loose. } 20. Rd3 Ne5 { Still better. }) (19... Rb8 { Slow. } 20. Rd3 b5 { Still better. }) (19... Kg7 { The wrong way. } 20. Rd3 b5 { Still better, but the king is not helping. }) 0-1


