{ His king side pawns are stuck. Your bishop on b2 wants the long diagonal open. Which pawn breaks through? }
16... g5 { He locks the king side. The h column is his, unless you break it open. [%csl Rg5] } 17. h4 { Pawn to h4! He takes, and g5 shuts his knight out. The bishop on b2 owns the long diagonal. [%cal Gh4g5,Gb2h8] } (17. h3 { Half a step, and nothing happens. } 17... Rh7 18. Rag1 Rdh8 { Only a little better. }) (17. Bd4 { The bishop hits the knight, and he steps aside. } 17... Ncd7 18. h4 Rxh4 { Only a little better. }) (17. Be5 { The bishop jumps in, and the rook hits h2. } 17... Rh3 18. Bg3 Re8 { Only a little better. }) (17. Rab1 { Doubling on a column with nothing on it. } 17... Rh3 18. Be5 Nfd7 { Equal. [%csl Rb1] }) (17. Bxf6 { You trade your best bishop. } 17... gxf6 18. d4 Nd7 { His king side is fine now. Worse. [%csl Rb2] }) 17... gxh4 { He takes. Now shut the door. } 18. g5 { Pawn to g5: the knight is kicked, and the h pawn is cut off behind it. Rooks to the h column next. [%csl Gg5][%cal Gh1h4] } (18. Rag1 { Also good, one move slower. } 18... Rdg8 19. g5 Nfd7 { The same. }) (18. Bd4 { You hit the knight first. } 18... Ncd7 19. g5 Ne8 { Fine, slower. }) (18. f4 { A pawn move. } 18... Rdg8 19. Rh2 Nfd7 { Only a little better. }) (18. Rh2 { The rook first. } 18... Rh7 19. Rah1 Rdh8 { Only a little better. }) 1-0


